  <section class="hero" id="top" data-file="mn-2026.kicad_sym" data-mode="Lisp Data" aria-labelledby="hero-name">
    <div class="hero-id">
      <h1 class="hero-name" id="hero-name">Michael Neuper</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">Computer science student at Stellenbosch University. I like low-level software, electronics and the maths underneath both, and I do almost all of it in Emacs.</p>
      <ul class="legend" aria-label="Colour key used across the site">
        <li data-cat="hw">hardware</li>
        <li data-cat="sw">software</li>
        <li data-cat="math">maths</li>
        <li data-cat="sec">security</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-chip">
      <figure class="pinout">
        <div class="chip-wrap"><!-- built from the list below --></div>
        <div class="pin-readout" aria-live="polite"><p class="dim">Point at a pin to read its function.</p></div>
        <figcaption>Figure 1. MN-2026, DIP-16 package, top view.</figcaption>
        <!-- Edit the pins here. data-cat: hw, sw, math or sec. "M-x some-command" in a description becomes a button. -->
        <ol class="pin-list">
          <li data-name="VCC" data-type="power">Supply input: coffee. Top it up with M-x coffee-refill.</li>
          <li data-name="C" data-type="I/O" data-cat="sw">Most of what I write, from a compiler to terminal toys like a spinning cube and the Game of Life.</li>
          <li data-name="ASM" data-type="I/O" data-cat="sw">Assembly, starting with the 8-bit computer’s eleven instructions.</li>
          <li data-name="JVM" data-type="output" data-cat="sw">The target of my compiler, so I read javap output for fun.</li>
          <li data-name="LINUX" data-type="I/O" data-cat="sw">Daily driver. Dotfiles on GitHub, and posts on packaging formats and turning a window manager into a desktop.</li>
          <li data-name="TTL" data-type="I/O" data-cat="hw">74-series logic. The 8-bit computer is dozens of these chips on breadboards.</li>
          <li data-name="SEC" data-type="input" data-cat="sec">Security: a consulting internship, a web application assessment, and the habit of asking how things break.</li>
          <li data-name="GND" data-type="power">Return path. Sleep, when the config is finished (it is never finished).</li>
          <li data-name="RST" data-type="input">Active low. Asserted by a walk without a laptop.</li>
          <li data-name="TA" data-type="output" data-cat="hw">Teaching assistant for introductory computer science and computer architecture.</li>
          <li data-name="TEX" data-type="output" data-cat="math">Notes, templates and maths typeset in LaTeX, usually from Emacs.</li>
          <li data-name="ORG" data-type="I/O" data-cat="sw">Org mode and org-roam for notes, literate configs, and replacing Jupyter.</li>
          <li data-name="EMACS" data-type="I/O" data-cat="sw">Doom Emacs, with a literate config of over a thousand lines. Location: ~/.config/emacs/.</li>
          <li data-name="MATH" data-type="input" data-cat="math">Project Euler, discrete maths, and modelling things in Python.</li>
          <li data-name="CAN" data-type="I/O">CAN bus, the network inside every car. Cars are my one interest that isn’t a computer, mostly.</li>
          <li data-name="NC" data-type="no connect">Reserved for the next obsession.</li>
        </ol>
