  <!-- ──────────────────────────── memory map ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec" id="projects" data-file="memory.map" data-mode="Fundamental">
    <p class="winbar"><span class="file">memory.map</span><span class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="meta" data-mm="meta"></span></p>
    <h2 class="h2">Projects</h2>
    <p class="lede">Laid out like a linker map: each project is a region in memory holding its one-line summary, and the hexdump shows exactly those bytes. Select one to read about it and try it out.</p>

    <div class="mm">
      <div class="mm-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="mm-cols">
        <div class="mm-table">
          <p class="mm-head" aria-hidden="true"><span>origin</span><span>length</span><span>name</span><span>source</span></p>
          <!-- Edit projects here. data-cat: hw, sw, math or sec. data-src: shown in the source column.
               data-demo: computer, scheduler, bytecode, othello or rbudp (optional); data-wide gives the
               demo the full width under the list. -->
          <ol class="mm-list">
            <li class="proj" data-cat="hw" data-src="built" data-demo="computer" data-wide>
              <h3 class="proj-name">8-bit-computer</h3>
              <p class="proj-sum">Ben Eater's 8-bit breadboard computer, built chip by chip.</p>
              <div class="proj-body">
                <p>My most recent finished project: a programmable computer on breadboards, built from 74-series logic chips following Ben Eater’s video series. It has 16 bytes of RAM, an 8-bit bus, and microcode in EEPROMs that drives sixteen control lines.</p>
                <p>The simulation below runs the same instruction set and microcode, so you can step through a program one control word at a time.</p>
                <p class="proj-stack">74HC logic, EEPROMs, breadboards, a lot of jumper wire</p>
                <p class="proj-links"><a href="https://eater.net/8bit">Ben Eater’s series</a></p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="proj" data-cat="sw" data-src="private" data-demo="scheduler">
              <h3 class="proj-name">os-scheduler</h3>
              <p class="proj-sum">A multicore operating system scheduler, for a concurrency course.</p>
              <div class="proj-body">
                <p>The part of an operating system that decides which process runs on which core, and for how long. I wrote it for a concurrency course, so the source stays private, but the idea fits in a small simulation: processes arrive, wait in a shared ready queue, and get dispatched to cores under first come, first served, round robin or preemptive priority scheduling.</p>
                <p class="proj-links dim">Source private (concurrency coursework)</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="proj" data-cat="sw" data-src="private" data-demo="othello">
              <h3 class="proj-name">othello-mcts</h3>
              <p class="proj-sum">An Othello player in C that runs Monte Carlo tree search across MPI processes.</p>
              <div class="proj-body">
                <p>The player picks a move by running thousands of random games from the current position and keeping the move that was explored most, using UCT to balance trying new moves against improving promising ones. Each move gets one second.</p>
                <p>The project was for a concurrency course, so the next step was to parallelise it with MPI, which I did in three ways: independent search trees combined at the end with <code>MPI_Allreduce</code>, trees that share their statistics every so often, and the same sharing done asynchronously with non-blocking sends. In ten-game matches on four processes, each version beat the one before it, except the asynchronous version, which won half its games against the synchronous one.</p>
                <p>You can play a serial version, rewritten in JavaScript for this page, below. While it thinks, each legal move is shaded by how often the search tried it.</p>
                <p class="proj-stack">C, MPI, Docker</p>
                <p class="proj-links dim">Source private (concurrency coursework)</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="proj" data-cat="sw" data-src="private" data-demo="bytecode">
              <h3 class="proj-name">jvm-compiler</h3>
              <p class="proj-sum">A compiler in C for a custom language that targets the JVM.</p>
              <div class="proj-body">
                <p>I wrote it in C for a computer architecture course. It reads a small custom programming language and produces bytecode the Java virtual machine can run. The source stays private, but the last stage is easy to show: the JVM is a stack machine, so an expression compiles to instructions that push and pop an operand stack. Type one below to see the bytecode and run it.</p>
                <p class="proj-stack">C, JVM bytecode</p>
                <p class="proj-links dim">Source private (computer architecture coursework)</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="proj" data-cat="sw" data-src="private" data-demo="rbudp">
              <h3 class="proj-name">chat-voip-p2p</h3>
              <p class="proj-sum">Four networking projects in Java: chat, fast UDP file transfer, voice calls and P2P sharing.</p>
              <div class="proj-body">
                <p>A series of networking projects built with my group for a university course:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>A chat server that handles many clients at once, with a JavaFX client, load-tested with dozens of simultaneous users.</li>
                  <li>File transfer over Reliable Blast UDP, measured against TCP across packet sizes, loss rates and file sizes from 1 to 100&nbsp;MB.</li>
                  <li>The chat extended with voice calls and group calls streamed over UDP, plus voice notes.</li>
                  <li>Peer-to-peer file sharing: a server brokers searches, then peers send files to each other directly over TCP, encrypted with AES-GCM keys exchanged using RSA, with pause and resume. We tested it across four machines over Tailscale.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Below is the RBUDP transfer in miniature: blast every packet over UDP, ask over TCP which ones arrived, and resend the gaps.</p>
                <p class="proj-stack">Java, JavaFX, TCP and UDP sockets, Java Sound, RSA, AES-GCM</p>
                <p class="proj-links dim">Source private (coursework, group project)</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <article class="mm-detail" aria-live="polite"></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────────── posts ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec" id="posts" data-file="posts.org" data-mode="Org">
